# Shell scripts must keep LF line endings in the working tree, on every platform.
#
# Why this matters here: .githooks/pre-push is the client-side guard against pushing
# to main. If git checks it out with CRLF (the default on Windows when core.autocrlf
# is true), the shebang line becomes "#!/bin/sh" and a POSIX shell reports
# "bad interpreter: No such file or directory". Git then treats the hook as failed to
# run, and on some paths simply skips it: a guard that looks installed but never
# blocks anything. Pinning eol=lf keeps the hook executable on Linux and macOS clones.
.githooks/**  text eol=lf
*.sh          text eol=lf

# The AppImage entrypoint and desktop file run on Linux, where a CRLF in the
# shebang breaks execution the same way.
AppRun        text eol=lf
*.desktop     text eol=lf

# JS served or bundled: normalize to LF for stable diffs and stable hashing.
*.mjs         text eol=lf
*.js          text eol=lf

# The BIP-39 English wordlist is hashed at runtime and compared against the SHA-256 of
# the list as published in bitcoin/bips (2048 lines, LF, trailing newline). A CRLF
# checkout would change every line and therefore the hash, so Bip39WordList.Load would
# fail on a Windows clone for a reason that has nothing to do with the contents. Pinned
# by exact path rather than by *.txt so no other text file inherits the rule by accident.
Slip39Demo.Core/Bip39/WordList.english.txt  text eol=lf

# The published BIP-39 vectors are kept byte for byte as upstream publishes them and
# their SHA-256 is asserted, so the suite can show it is running the real vectors and not
# a copy someone adjusted until the tests went green. Same CRLF reasoning as above.
Slip39Demo.Tests/Bip39/Vectors/**  text eol=lf

# The published BIP-32 vectors, vendored as the BIP text and hashed by Bip32VectorTests.
# Same CRLF reasoning as the two entries above.
Slip39Demo.Tests/Bip32/Vectors/**  text eol=lf
